2026-09-13 21:32:57 slice 32: EH flake root cause found and fixed. The native
backend's _zan_rt_eh_state emitted calloc(16+256*1024, 0) -- calloc with
size=0 -- so libmalloc handed back a minimal 16-byte chunk instead of the
262 160-byte state block. Every setjmp write into bufs[k] (= state+16 and
onward, ~190 bytes of jmp_buf plus the fp/magic stamps) then overran the
heap: adjacent chunk payloads and malloc metadata were smashed, later
malloc(32) calls (int2str) received pointers inside the live state block and
sprintf wrote decimal digits straight into the armed jmp_buf, and recycled
chunks overwrote bufs[0] word 0 (the x19 slot) so longjmp restored garbage
callee-saved registers -- the wild-PC / hang / PAC-auth flakes at ~30-60% of
runs. Diagnosis: multi-cycle repro program (spin2), lldb ATTACH-mode hardware
watchpoints on state+16/state+432 caught the writer red-handed
(_platform_memmove <- __sfvwrite <- sprintf <- zan_rt_int2str), and a
malloc_size probe proved the "state block" was 16 bytes. Fix: calloc(n, 1)
plus an OOM check mirroring zan_rt_alloc. Verified: exceptions_basic 40/40,
exceptions_typed_catch 40/40, spin2 12/12 (previously ~30-50% SIGSEGV),
kernels 27/27, fixed point gen2==gen3 at 6 335 999 bytes. A/B against the
slice-31 compiler confirmed the enum_tostring address-printing gap is
pre-existing (queued as slice 33).
